# DKMS installation

DKMS rebuilds out-of-tree kernel modules when a new kernel is installed.

This driver supports Linux 6.1 and newer.

Install DKMS and the headers for the running kernel:

```sh
sudo apt install dkms linux-headers-$(uname -r)
```

Register this checkout as `crystalhd/3.10.0`. The `dkms add` command is needed
only once; omit it when `dkms status` already lists that version.

```sh
sudo ln -sfn "$PWD" /usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.0
dkms status
sudo dkms add -m crystalhd -v 3.10.0
sudo dkms build -m crystalhd -v 3.10.0 -k "$(uname -r)"
sudo dkms install -m crystalhd -v 3.10.0 -k "$(uname -r)"
```

Use `--force` on `dkms build` and `dkms install` only when intentionally
replacing an existing build of the same version. Verify the result:

```sh
dkms status
modinfo -n crystalhd
modinfo -F srcversion crystalhd
modinfo -F srcversion ./driver/linux/crystalhd.ko
```

The installed and checkout source versions should match. If an older module is
already loaded, first make sure no process owns the device, then reload it
without force:

```sh
sudo fuser -v /dev/crystalhd || true
sudo rmmod crystalhd
sudo modprobe crystalhd
```

Never force-unload an active CrystalHD module. Reboot instead if a normal
`rmmod` reports that the module is busy.

Once registered, DKMS automatically builds the module for newly installed
kernels. Firmware, userspace libraries, VA-API, GStreamer, and browser files
are installed separately by `sudo make install`.
